This dissertation focuses on UAV-based monitoring of cotton maturation during the harvest-aid stage in Xinjiang, with defoliation rate and boll opening rate as the two key agronomic indicators. To address the practical challenges of field investigation, including complex backgrounds, dynamic illumination, low efficiency of manual sampling, and limited deployability of existing methods, the study develops an end-to-end technical pipeline that spans dataset construction, interpretable modeling, lightweight recognition, low-light data generation, all-weather perception, and field validation. The goal is to transform cotton maturation status from an experience-based judgment target into a computable, verifiable, and operationally usable remote-sensing phenotype.

First, a dataset of cotton defoliation rate and boll opening rate covering multiple acquisition periods, diverse illumination conditions, and complex field backgrounds is established. The study further standardizes image acquisition, manual ground-truth statistics, position parsing, sample organization, and evaluation protocols, providing a consistent data foundation for subsequent experiments. Second, vectorized RGB pixel values are used to establish engineering baselines with PCA plus logistic regression and random-forest variants, and Accuracy and Macro-F1 are used to analyze dual-indicator recognition under class imbalance. On this basis, a structured-feature scheme involving spectral indices, foreground segmentation, morphology, and texture is analyzed as a direction for subsequent interpretable modeling and experimental validation.

To satisfy edge-deployment constraints, a lightweight dual-indicator recognition model, RTCMNet (Real-Time Cotton Monitoring Network), is proposed. The model integrates a shared backbone, multi-scale convolutional attention, and dual classification heads, achieving a five-fold cross-validation mean accuracy of $0.93 plus.minus 0.02$ across the two tasks with only 0.35 M parameters and a per-image inference latency of 32 ms on edge devices, thereby enabling real-time joint recognition of defoliation rate and boll opening rate. To further address low-light and complex illumination scenarios, the dissertation develops a paired low-light data generation pipeline inspired by physical imaging and ISP mechanisms. In the RGB domain, the pipeline jointly simulates exposure attenuation, white-balance drift, Poisson-Gaussian noise, mild blur, and vignetting, and uses real nighttime UAV images for statistical calibration, thus providing controllable supervision for subsequent enhancement and robust recognition.

On top of this data foundation, a lightweight enhancement front-end, $L^3$-AgriUAVNet, is designed and coupled with the dual-indicator recognition model to form an all-weather recognition pipeline. Built upon the HVI color space and a lightweight RFDN-S backbone, the model contains only 0.139 M parameters and 6.252 GMACs, attains the best SSIM (0.893) among all compared methods on the paired test set, reaches a PSNR of 26.24 dB on par with heavy Transformer models, and runs at 202 ms per image (4.95 FPS) on an iPad-class device. The results show that the enhancement module improves luminance distribution, structural restoration, and color stability. On real sunset cotton-field imagery, the defoliation-rate and boll-opening-rate predictions before and after enhancement exhibit distributional changes associated with task type and harvest-aid stage, demonstrating the feasibility of cascading $L^3$-AgriUAVNet with RTCMNet. Because synchronous manual ground truth was unavailable, this experiment was not used to quantify gains in recognition accuracy. In downstream seedling detection, enhanced inputs raise the mAP\@0.5 of the lightweight edge detector YOLOv8n by 14.6% (from 0.1393 to 0.1596), while the marginal benefit on stronger detectors is limited, indicating that the gains of the enhancement front-end are concentrated in lightweight edge-side pipelines. Finally, the complete pipeline is embedded into real field operations. Through multi-temporal UAV acquisition, online recognition, geographic mapping, and manual verification, the spatial distribution and temporal evolution of cotton maturation are systematically analyzed. The results demonstrate that the proposed method can stably identify delayed-maturation zones, local anomalous areas, and maturation progression regions, suggesting its applicability in real agricultural scenarios.

This work establishes a systematic research path from data and algorithms to field-level validation for cotton maturation monitoring. The study provides a useful reference for intelligent monitoring of cotton maturation, harvest-aid decision support, and robust visual perception in agricultural environments with dynamic illumination.
